1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,920
This bottle holds ethanol.

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:04,320
It could have started as sugar, fermented by

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:04,880
yeast.

4
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:07,680
It could have started as ethene, a gas

5
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:09,920
made from crude oil or natural gas.

6
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,400
Or, in a lab, it could come from

7
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:15,920
a haloalkane, like bromoethane, heated in a flask.

8
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,400
Same molecule every time.

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,200
So how can we make ethanol, and which

10
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:22,240
way would you choose?

11
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,960
That depends on what you start with, the

12
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:27,520
conditions each route needs, and how you get

13
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:29,280
the ethanol out at the end.

14
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,820
Start with the route you've already seen.

15
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,940
In the addition video, ethene and steam made

16
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:39,300
ethanol, with a phosphoric acid catalyst, about three

17
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:42,340
hundred degrees and high pressure, about sixty to

18
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:44,220
seventy atmospheres in industry.

19
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:48,260
Remember, it's reversible, so it's an equilibrium, written

20
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:49,700
with two half arrows.

21
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:53,540
Two gas molecules become one, so high pressure

22
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,300
pushes it towards ethanol.

23
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:59,260
Two more conditions matter for judging this route.

24
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:02,740
The catalyst speeds up both directions equally, so

25
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:05,700
equilibrium comes sooner, but it doesn't move the

26
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:07,060
equilibrium.

27
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,380
And the temperature is a compromise.

28
00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:12,340
The forward reaction releases heat, so a lower

29
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:15,460
temperature would give more ethanol at equilibrium, but

30
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:16,660
far too slowly.

31
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:20,266
Even so, only a few percent of the

32
00:01:20,347 --> 00:01:23,226
ethene reacts on each pass through the reactor.

33
00:01:23,467 --> 00:01:25,386
So the gas mixture is cooled.

34
00:01:25,867 --> 00:01:28,666
Ethanol and water condense to a liquid, while

35
00:01:28,747 --> 00:01:31,306
ethene stays a gas, and it's sent back

36
00:01:31,387 --> 00:01:33,786
through the reactor, again and again.

37
00:01:34,427 --> 00:01:36,666
The ethanol is then separated from the water

38
00:01:36,747 --> 00:01:38,026
by distillation.

39
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:41,066
The plant runs nonstop, so it's fast and

40
00:01:41,147 --> 00:01:44,266
continuous, but the ethene comes from crude oil

41
00:01:44,347 --> 00:01:45,546
or natural gas.

42
00:01:46,767 --> 00:01:49,326
Route two starts with a haloalkane.

43
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:53,566
Heat bromoethane with aqueous sodium hydroxide, meaning it's

44
00:01:53,607 --> 00:01:56,366
dissolved in water, and the bromine is swapped

45
00:01:56,407 --> 00:01:59,166
for an O H, giving ethanol and sodium

46
00:01:59,247 --> 00:02:00,286
bromide.

47
00:02:00,367 --> 00:02:01,486
Here's why.

48
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:04,446
Bromine pulls electrons from the carbon it's on,

49
00:02:04,527 --> 00:02:07,086
so that carbon is slightly positive, and the

50
00:02:07,287 --> 00:02:10,206
negative hydroxide ion is attracted to it.

51
00:02:10,247 --> 00:02:12,686
The hydroxide bonds to that carbon, and the

52
00:02:12,727 --> 00:02:15,406
bromine leaves as a bromide ion.

53
00:02:15,447 --> 00:02:17,526
It's a substitution, like the swap in the

54
00:02:17,567 --> 00:02:20,606
alkane video, but this time the halogen is

55
00:02:20,647 --> 00:02:21,806
the atom pushed out.

56
00:02:23,034 --> 00:02:24,633
Why heat it under reflux?

57
00:02:25,114 --> 00:02:28,473
Heating speeds the reaction up, but bromoethane boils

58
00:02:28,554 --> 00:02:31,353
at thirty eight degrees and ethanol at seventy

59
00:02:31,434 --> 00:02:33,673
eight, so they'd escape as vapour.

60
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:36,953
The upright condenser cools those vapours, and they

61
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:38,473
drip back into the flask.

62
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:41,393
Afterwards, the alcohol is distilled out of the

63
00:02:41,434 --> 00:02:41,913
mixture.

64
00:02:42,394 --> 00:02:44,313
And the O H lands exactly where the

65
00:02:44,394 --> 00:02:49,033
halogen was: two-bromopropane gives propan-two-ol.

66
00:02:49,274 --> 00:02:51,753
So this route can make a particular alcohol,

67
00:02:51,834 --> 00:02:52,953
not just ethanol.

68
00:02:54,167 --> 00:02:55,766
Route three is the oldest.

69
00:02:56,327 --> 00:03:00,486
Yeast ferments glucose: one glucose makes two ethanol

70
00:03:00,607 --> 00:03:02,246
and two carbon dioxide.

71
00:03:02,487 --> 00:03:05,926
Check it: six carbons, twelve hydrogens and six

72
00:03:06,007 --> 00:03:08,006
oxygens on each side.

73
00:03:08,127 --> 00:03:10,806
Enzymes inside the yeast are the catalyst, so

74
00:03:10,847 --> 00:03:12,806
the yeast goes on the arrow, not in

75
00:03:12,887 --> 00:03:13,686
the equation.

76
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:16,260
Here's a school setup.

77
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,300
Glucose solution and yeast go in a flask,

78
00:03:19,340 --> 00:03:21,300
in a warm water bath at about thirty

79
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:22,740
five degrees.

80
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:24,580
That's warm enough for the enzymes to work

81
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:27,220
quickly, but not so hot that it damages

82
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:28,020
them.

83
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:30,660
A bung and delivery tube keep air out,

84
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:34,500
because with oxygen, yeast can respire aerobically, breaking

85
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:36,900
some sugar down fully to carbon dioxide and

86
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,860
water, so less ethanol forms.

87
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:42,660
The tube also lets gas escape, so pressure

88
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:44,100
can't build up.

89
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:46,820
Bubble it through limewater, and the limewater turns

90
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:49,140
milky, showing carbon dioxide.

91
00:03:50,367 --> 00:03:53,206
After a few days, the bubbling slows.

92
00:03:53,407 --> 00:03:57,166
Fermentation usually stops at around fifteen percent ethanol,

93
00:03:57,247 --> 00:03:59,886
because that much ethanol poisons the yeast.

94
00:04:00,127 --> 00:04:02,846
To get more concentrated ethanol, filter out the

95
00:04:02,927 --> 00:04:04,686
yeast and distil the mixture.

96
00:04:05,087 --> 00:04:07,966
Ethanol boils at seventy eight degrees and water

97
00:04:08,127 --> 00:04:10,446
at one hundred, so the vapour that comes

98
00:04:10,607 --> 00:04:12,206
off is richer in ethanol.

99
00:04:12,607 --> 00:04:15,806
Industry uses fractional distillation to go further.

100
00:04:17,034 --> 00:04:18,713
So which route is best?

101
00:04:19,194 --> 00:04:21,353
Compare them one feature at a time.

102
00:04:21,834 --> 00:04:24,953
Hydration is fast and continuous, but it needs

103
00:04:25,034 --> 00:04:28,633
high temperature and pressure, and a non-renewable feedstock.

104
00:04:29,034 --> 00:04:32,473
Fermentation runs warm, not hot, from a renewable

105
00:04:32,554 --> 00:04:35,593
sugar crop, but it's slow, its dilute product

106
00:04:35,754 --> 00:04:38,313
takes energy to distil, and growing the crop

107
00:04:38,394 --> 00:04:39,673
takes land and water.

108
00:04:40,154 --> 00:04:43,033
Substitution suits making a particular alcohol in the

109
00:04:43,114 --> 00:04:45,673
lab, but the haloalkane has to be made

110
00:04:45,834 --> 00:04:46,313
first.

111
00:04:46,794 --> 00:04:48,553
So there's no single winner.

112
00:04:48,874 --> 00:04:51,273
It depends on the feedstock, the energy, and

113
00:04:51,354 --> 00:04:52,633
the purity you need.

114
00:04:53,834 --> 00:04:55,833
So, three routes to ethanol.

115
00:04:56,234 --> 00:05:00,313
Hydration: ethene and steam, hot, under pressure, with

116
00:05:00,394 --> 00:05:01,833
an acid catalyst.

117
00:05:02,154 --> 00:05:07,433
Substitution: a haloalkane with aqueous hydroxide, under reflux.

118
00:05:07,754 --> 00:05:12,073
Fermentation: glucose with yeast, warm, no air.

119
00:05:12,394 --> 00:05:14,713
Then, for each one, ask how you'd get

120
00:05:14,794 --> 00:05:15,913
the ethanol out.

121
00:05:17,134 --> 00:05:18,053
Your turn.

122
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:21,213
A student keeps two flasks of glucose solution

123
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:23,933
and yeast at thirty five degrees.

124
00:05:24,014 --> 00:05:27,133
Flask A has a bung and delivery tube.

125
00:05:27,214 --> 00:05:29,613
Air is bubbled through flask B the whole

126
00:05:29,814 --> 00:05:30,653
time.

127
00:05:30,734 --> 00:05:33,853
After two days, flask B holds less ethanol

128
00:05:33,934 --> 00:05:35,453
than flask A.

129
00:05:35,534 --> 00:05:38,253
Explain this difference, and write a balanced equation

130
00:05:38,294 --> 00:05:40,653
for the fermentation of glucose.

131
00:05:40,814 --> 00:05:42,013
Pause here to answer.

132
00:05:52,434 --> 00:05:55,233
Air was bubbled through flask B, so its

133
00:05:55,274 --> 00:05:56,633
yeast had oxygen.

134
00:05:56,914 --> 00:06:00,673
With oxygen, yeast can respire aerobically, breaking some

135
00:06:00,754 --> 00:06:03,793
sugar down fully to carbon dioxide and water,

136
00:06:03,914 --> 00:06:05,793
so less ethanol forms.

137
00:06:06,114 --> 00:06:07,953
The temperature was the same in both.

138
00:06:08,514 --> 00:06:09,153
The equation:

139
00:06:09,714 --> 00:06:12,833
C six H twelve O six gives two

140
00:06:13,154 --> 00:06:15,793
C two H five O H, plus two

141
00:06:16,034 --> 00:06:16,753
C O two.

142
00:06:17,714 --> 00:06:20,393
Yeast as a reactant is the classic slip.

143
00:06:20,594 --> 00:06:23,313
Its enzymes are the catalyst, so yeast goes

144
00:06:23,354 --> 00:06:24,113
on the arrow.

145
00:06:25,334 --> 00:06:25,973
Last one.

146
00:06:26,614 --> 00:06:30,373
One-bromobutane is heated under reflux with aqueous sodium

147
00:06:30,454 --> 00:06:31,253
hydroxide.

148
00:06:31,814 --> 00:06:34,613
Write a balanced equation for the reaction, name

149
00:06:34,654 --> 00:06:37,333
the organic product, and explain why the mixture

150
00:06:37,414 --> 00:06:39,213
is heated under reflux.

151
00:06:39,494 --> 00:06:40,773
Pause here to answer.

152
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:53,680
The hydroxide swaps for the bromine, on the

153
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:54,560
same carbon.

154
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:57,440
C H three, C H two, C H

155
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:00,360
two, C H two Br, plus Na O

156
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:03,520
H, gives C H three, C H two,

157
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,000
C H two, C H two O H,

158
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,760
plus Na Br.

159
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:10,320
The product is butan-one-ol.

160
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:12,960
Heating speeds it up, and reflux stops the

161
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:17,120
volatile liquids escaping: the condenser returns their vapours.

162
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:19,920
Writing butan-two-ol is the slip.

163
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:22,160
The O H takes the halogen's place.

164
00:07:23,367 --> 00:07:25,766
So ethanol can come from crude oil or

165
00:07:25,847 --> 00:07:29,206
natural gas, or from plants, and each route

166
00:07:29,327 --> 00:07:32,166
sets its own conditions and its own separation.

